course: religion-ethics
lesson: 66
title: Grief: crying, not crying and feeling numb
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Grief is the big, heavy feeling that comes when someone we love dies; you could call it love that has nowhere to go for a while.
explain: It does not look the same in everyone: Ellie cries every night for a week after Grandad's funeral, her cousin Tom does not cry at all when his nan dies, and both of them loved their grandparents.
explain: Some people feel numb, as if they were in a bubble; some feel it in the body, with tummy aches or nights without sleep; and tears can arrive a month later, in the middle of a maths lesson, out of nowhere.
explain: Grief comes in waves, like the sea at the beach: a big wave, then calm, then another wave when a song or a smell brings it back, and over time the waves usually get gentler.
explain: Christians remember that Jesus wept when his friend Lazarus died, and people of every faith and none cry for those they love; boys and men cry too.
explain: The wise move is the same for everyone: let the feeling come in its own way, and tell a grown-up you trust how you feel.
fact: Grief is the mix of feelings that comes when someone we love dies.
fact: Crying is a normal and healthy way to let sadness out; it usually gets less over time, though it can come back.
fact: Not crying does not mean not loving; some people cry later, cry inside, or show sadness in other ways.
fact: Feeling numb, «like being in a bubble», is very common after a death, especially at first; it is the mind's way of coping with a big shock.
fact: Grief often comes in waves: something small, like a song or a smell, can bring it back suddenly, even after a good day.
fact: Grief can show in the body as tummy aches, headaches, tiredness or trouble sleeping; talking to someone, reading or calm music before bed can help, and if aches do not get better, a doctor can help.
fact: Boys and men cry too; crying is human, not girly and not weak.
fact: In the Bible, Jesus wept for his friend Lazarus, so Christians see crying as part of love.
fact: Having fun, like going to a mate's birthday party, does not mean you have stopped caring.
fact: Writing a letter to someone who has died can help you say what you did not get to say; the letter is for you.
fact: In the American South «fixin' to» means «about to», and «y'all» means «you all».
fact: There is no single right way to grieve, and no feeling in grief is «wrong».
exercise: Ellie cries every night for a week after Grandad's funeral. What is true? | It is a normal, healthy way to let sadness out | It means something is badly wrong with her and she needs a doctor | She should be made to stop at once | It means she will go on crying like this every night for ever
exercise: Tom didn't cry when his nan died, and his cousin says he can't have loved her. What is true? | Not crying doesn't mean not loving | Love always shows itself in lots of tears at the funeral | Tom must secretly have been glad that his nan died | Only people who cry in front of others really care
exercise: Sienna says she feels «nothing», like being in a bubble. What is this? | A common way the mind copes with a big shock | A sign she never cared about her uncle | An illness that needs hospital straight away | A feeling only grown-ups can have
exercise: Jake from Texas says, «I'm fixin' to cry but I can't, y'all.» What does he mean? | He's about to cry, but the tears won't come | He is going to mend something at home that got broken | He wants everybody else to cry | He has finished all his crying now and feels completely fine
exercise: If tears won't come, what else can let sadness out? | Talking, drawing, being quiet or playing | Nothing, the sadness stays stuck for ever | Only shouting at the people around you | Pretending that nobody has died at all
exercise: Why might Lily suddenly cry in maths a month after her mum died? | Grief comes in waves and can return without warning | She is upset only about the hard sum | She is pretending, to get out of the lesson | Grief lasts a month, so it must be something else
exercise: What can Lily do when grief hits her in class? | Ask to step out and have a minute | Hide it and never tell anyone at all | Walk out of school and go home alone | Tell herself that crying is not allowed
exercise: Oscar's dad says boys shouldn't cry; Grandma disagrees. Who is right? | Grandma: boys and men cry too | Dad: crying makes boys weak | Dad: only girls feel real sadness | Neither: nobody should ever cry
exercise: Who in the Bible wept when his friend died? | Jesus, for his friend Lazarus | Peter, for his friend Judas | Noah, for the people of the flood | David, for the giant Goliath
exercise: Mia's tummy has hurt every day since Grandad died. What could be going on? | Grief can make the body feel ill | She caught Grandad's illness from him | Tummy aches never come from feelings | She is making it up for attention
exercise: What should Mia do about the tummy aches? | Tell a grown-up, and see a doctor if they don't ease | Keep quiet so that nobody worries about her | Stop eating until the aches go away | Wait a whole year before telling anyone
exercise: Is it okay for Charlie to go to his mate's party while he's sad about Grandma? | Yes, fun doesn't mean he has stopped caring | No, he must not smile or have fun for a whole year | No, parties are forbidden for families after a funeral | Only if he promises his mum not to enjoy it one bit
exercise: Holly can't sleep, thinking about Grandad. What might help? | Talking before bed, reading or calm music | Staying up all night on her phone in bed | Lying awake and telling nobody at all about it | Watching a scary film late at night to tire herself out
exercise: Freddie writes a letter to his grandad who died and puts it under his pillow. What is it for? | To say what he feels and didn't get to say | So Grandad can come at night and read it | To test whether ghosts can read letters | It shows that something is wrong with him
exercise: How many right ways are there to grieve? | No single one; people grieve differently | Exactly one: crying hard every single day | Two: crying a lot, or else being very angry | None: grieving at all is always a big mistake
exercise: How does grief usually change over time? | It comes in waves that usually get gentler | It stops completely on the day of the funeral | It stays the same strong pain every minute | It turns into nothing after one week
test: «bruv, Tom never even cried at his nan's funeral, he's well cold innit,» says a boy in London (bruv = mate). Fair answer? | Not crying isn't the same as not caring | He's right; if there are no tears, there was no love | Tom must have been happy she died | Tom should be made to cry properly at the next one
test: «Sienna's acting mad sus since her uncle died, like a robot, no cap,» says a girl in Ohio (sus = fishy, no cap = no lie). What's the kind reading? | Numbness is a normal reaction to a shock | She is hiding something bad about him | She never liked her uncle at all | She is ill and needs an ambulance
test: A teacher says formally, «Grief is frequently experienced in waves.» Which scene fits best? | Feeling fine, then crying when a song plays | Crying once at the funeral, never again | Feeling exactly the same, heavy sadness every single minute | Feeling happy all the time after a death, as if nothing happened
test: «Ma wee bairn's been greetin' every nicht since her grandda went,» says Ellie's Scottish aunt (bairn = child, greetin' = crying). What should the family do? | Let her cry and stay close to her | Tell her to stop greetin' at once | Send her to bed early as a punishment | Pretend not to notice anything
test: «Stop blubbing, boys don't cry, sort yourself out,» snaps an older cousin at Oscar. What is true? | Boys and men cry too; it's human, not weak | The cousin is right; tears are really only for girls | Oscar should go and hide until he has stopped crying | Crying only makes the sadness last much, much longer
test: What would happen if Holly lay awake every night and told nobody? | She'd stay tired and alone with it; telling helps | The feelings would vanish by themselves | She would forget her grandad completely | Her grandad would come back to visit her
test: In a cartoon, a girl's grandad dies, and in the next scene she laughs and never mentions him again. What's more like real life? | Feelings come and go for a long time | Everyone forgets after one day | Laughing proves she didn't love him | Real people never laugh after a death
test: «Our kid's had a belly ache every day since me grandad died, and he's not even sick,» says a girl from Liverpool (our kid = my brother). Best answer? | Grief can show in the body; tell a grown-up | He must be faking it to get days off school | Belly aches are never, ever linked to feelings | He should eat a lot less until the ache stops
test: Myth to check: «If you go to a party a week after a death, you didn't really love the person.» | False: fun and sadness can live together | True: you must stay sad for a year | True: parties are banned for mourners | False, but only if you go to church first
test: «Writing to grandad when he can't read it is lowkey delulu,» says Freddie's big sister (lowkey = a bit, delulu = deluded). Fair answer? | The letter is for Freddie, to let feelings out | She's right; it is pointless and a bit silly, really | Grandad will read it while Freddie sleeps | It has to be posted to the cemetery, or it won't work
test: «Ah'm proper mardy and ah keep crying, is summat wrong wi' me?» asks Leo from Yorkshire (mardy = grumpy, summat = something). Best answer? | Nothing's wrong; grief can make you grumpy and teary | Yes, he should be taken to hospital straight away tonight | Yes, crying this much is dangerous for a boy his age | He should hide his feelings from everyone, even his mum
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression of grief following a bereavement? | Its expression varies between individuals, and no single form is correct | It is invariably expressed through weeping on the day of the funeral | Its absence of tears demonstrates an absence of affection | It is confined to adults and is not experienced by children
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a bereaved child may weep without warning some weeks after a death. | Grief tends to recur in waves, frequently prompted by small reminders | The child is feigning distress in order to avoid lessons | Grief is customarily concluded within a period of one month | Weeping after the funeral is indicative of physical illness
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning bodily symptoms during bereavement. | Grief may occasion headaches, abdominal pain and disturbed sleep | Grief is a purely mental state having no bodily effects | Abdominal pain in bereavement is invariably feigned | Disturbed sleep in bereavement requires immediate admission to hospital
exam: Whereas a parent asserts that boys ought not to weep, ascertain which response is consistent with the matters taught. | Weeping is a human response proper to persons of either sex | Weeping is a sign of weakness proper only to girls and to small children | Weeping is prohibited by every religious tradition | Weeping is permissible only within a place of worship
